      "side": 1,
      "message": "Nit: I\u0027d usually suggest doing\n```\nlet mut assigned_port: c_uint \u003d 0;\n// ...\n\u0026mut assigned_port,\n// ...\nOk((server, assigned_port as u32))\n```\n\nfor two reasons:\n1. Some platforms have a 16-bit integer (unlikely this code would run there, but...). For these platforms, whether or not what you\u0027re doing works depends on the endianness of the system.\n2. Platforms are allowed to have an integer larger than 32-bits. (Not actually aware of any, but spec doesn\u0027t prevent this) On these platforms, `ARpcServer_newVsock` may overwrite other memory in Rust\n\nBy making sure that pointers match *exactly* the `c_foo` type and then doing a cast on a value, you ensure that the worst that can happen is truncation.",
